  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 258 AM EDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nToday-Tonight...Low offshore the Mid-Atlantic coast strengthens \ntoday, pulling a front southward into north Florida where it will \nstall near to just north of the area. Scattered to numerous showers \nand storms (rain chances 40-70%) forecast with the inland moving \nsea breeze this afternoon, as a very moist airmass remains in \nplace (PW values 2-2.3\"). This deeper moisture will keep DCAPE \nvalues rather low, around 500 J/kg, and temps aloft are relatively\nwarm, around -5 to -6C at 500 mb, so strong storm threat remains \nlow. However, can't rule out gusty winds up to 40-50 mph and \nfrequent lightning with a few storms. More likely hazard from \nstorms or heavier showers will be locally heavy rainfall of 1-3\" \nwhich may produce minor flooding, as steering winds will be weak. \nConvection diminishes this evening, but isolated to scattered \nonshore moving showers and a storm or two will be possible, mainly\nnorth of the Treasure Coast into tonight.\n\nSeasonably warm and humid conditions continue today, with highs in \nthe mid to upper 80s at the coast and upper 80s to low 90s inland. \nPeak heat index values will range from 98-102F. Overnight lows will \nbe in the low to mid 70s. \n  \nThursday-Friday...Mid/upper level trough digs southward across the \neastern U.S. into late week, as surface low offshore the eastern \nU.S. continues to deepen and lift northward. This will shift the \nfront southward across the area tomorrow. Breezy to windy N/NE winds \ndevelop behind the front tomorrow and continue into late week, which \nwill lead to hazardous boating and beach conditions. Sustained wind \nspeeds of 15-25 mph with gusts up to 30-35 mph are forecast north of \nOkeechobee County and the Treasure Coast tomorrow, with highest wind \nspeeds/gusts focused at the coast. Sustained winds 15-20 mph, with \ngusts 25-30 mph then spread southward on Friday. Scattered to \nnumerous showers and storms forecast again on Thursday (PoPs 40-60%) \nwith passing front. Drier air will then lower rain chances into \nFriday, with only low end rain chances (20-30%) focused along the \ncoast.   \n\nHighs in the mid to upper 80s on Friday, drop just slightly Friday \ninto the low to mid 80s for much of the area. Overnight lows will \nremain in the 70s Thursday night and then as drier/cooler air works \ninto the area will see lows in the mid to upper 60s for much of the \nregion Friday night. Some coastal locations near to south of the \nCape, however, may remain in the low 70s.  \n\nSaturday-Tuesday...Drier and slightly cooler conditions forecast \ninto the weekend through early next week, giving us our first taste \nof Fall. While highs still reach into the 80s, overnight lows drop \ninto the 60s for the first time since early to mid June (see climate \nsection for last dates below). Noticeably lower humidity will \nalso make conditions more comfortable through the period. Mostly \ndry conditions forecast this weekend, with a gradually increase in\nrain chances through Tuesday as moisture slowly builds. Breezy \nN/NE winds may continue along the coast into Saturday, but winds \nsubside through Sunday and into early next week.",
